/// A mod menu type's part.
pub trait MenuPart: Send + Sync + 'static {
#[doc(hidden)] fn menu_subscribed(&self) -> &'static [Option<HookId>];
/// Slot placement and stack limits for registered vanilla and mod menus;
/// mounts use `horizon:mount_inventory`, while player inventory and the
/// creative picker have no registered type. `slot` is the backing index,
/// including player inventory slots. The slot's own answers arrive first;
/// the maximum is bounded to 1–99 and the item limit. Refusal also prevents
/// quick-move top-ups. Runs on server and client prediction with the read
/// `Host`: it is a query, so it can neither write nor cancel. A click asks
/// once for each slot and stack it consults while it plans, and never again
/// when it validates or applies. Answer from the stack and the host alone,
/// and fast. On a LAN host's own click the LAN host is not reachable
/// through the `Host` (it answers as if no LAN were open).
/// `container` is the container `slot` indexes ([`MenuContainer`]): a mod
/// crafting grid's slots are `Grid`'s and its result `Result`'s slot 0.
/// `context` says who and where the ask is about ([`MenuSlotContext`]):
/// the player whose menu it is, the menu's block entity and a read-only
/// copy of the menu's own containers' slots, keyed by the same container
/// (`context.slot(container, index)`), built once per click plan and
/// filled the same on the server and on the client's prediction. Parts that read `context.setting(..)` or `side()` have no
/// counterpart on the Host. Named injections request `hook_version = 4`.
/// Side: both.
fn menu_slot_rules(&self, host: &dyn Host, event: &mut MenuSlotRulesEvent<'_>) { let _ = host;let _ = event;  }
/// Whether a player's block menu of a mod menu type stays open
/// (`AbstractContainerMenu.stillValid`). Asked on the server each tick a
/// player has the menu of the block at `pos` open, and before each click
/// on it. `valid` arrives as vanilla's answer: the block entity is there
/// and `eye` is within the player's block interaction range plus 4 of each
/// block behind the menu; a part that leaves it alone keeps that answer.
/// Set it to keep the menu open or close it. Vanilla menu types are never
/// asked, and a menu whose block entity is gone closes without asking.
/// Runs in the restricted context; answer from the event and context
/// alone, and fast.
/// Side: server.
fn menu_still_valid(&self, context: &dyn LoopContext, event: &mut MenuStillValidEvent<'_>) { let _ = context;let _ = event;  }
/// A recipe viewer's "+" on a mod menu (JEI's `IRecipeTransferHandler`):
/// move one set of `recipe`'s ingredients (as many as the items allow
/// with `max`) from `player`'s inventory into the open menu of a mod menu
/// type, or with `dry_run` only say what that would do. Asked on the
/// server before the game's own grid transfer, only for a mod menu type
/// whose part answers, with `menu` its type, `pos` its block (None for a
/// menu with no block) and `recipe` the recipe's id as the viewer sent
/// it. Return `Some(Moves)` once moved, or `Missing`, `NoRoom` or
/// `NotIntoMenu`; a part that returns None lets the game try its own
/// crafting-grid transfer. A dry run must move nothing.
/// Side: server.
///
/// Return `Some` to set `result`; the first part returning `Some` wins.
fn menu_transfer(&self, host: &mut dyn ServerHost, event: &mut MenuTransferEvent<'_>) -> Option < RecipeTransferResult > { let _ = host;let _ = event; None }
}
/// A mod world fluid's part.
pub trait FluidPart: Send + Sync + 'static {
#[doc(hidden)] fn fluid_subscribed(&self) -> &'static [Option<HookId>];
/// How a mod's world fluid flows at `pos`, starting from what it declared (NeoForge's
/// `BaseFlowingFluid` and `FluidType` getters: `getTickDelay`, `getDropOff`,
/// `getSlopeFindDistance`, `canConvertToSource`). `fluid` is the id it was declared with.
/// Asked for every flow step and every cell a spread looks at: answer fast. An answer
/// outside what `WorldFluid` accepts is brought back within it. Server-side.
/// Side: server.
fn fluid_rules(&self, level: &dyn LevelHost, event: &mut FluidRulesEvent<'_>) { let _ = level;let _ = event;  }
}
/// An entity type's part (arismoko/horizon#1077): code attached through
/// `RegistrationContext::entity_part` to one entity type, a mod's own or vanilla's
/// (`minecraft:zombie`). Its `register_goals` runs at every `Mob` construction of that
/// type, spawned or loaded, right after the species' own goals — vanilla's `registerGoals`
/// moment — and the goals it adds join the mob's two selectors beside vanilla's, same
/// priorities and interruption. Its `register_behaviors` runs wherever a brain-driven
/// species' brain is built (#824). Its `spawn_cluster_size` answers how large a spawn group of the
/// type may grow. Its `general_entity_tick` runs for one general entity row's authoritative
/// tick (#792). Annotate each part trait implementation with
/// [`horizon_part`] to derive its subscriptions; the goal, behavior and spawn-size methods
/// are not hooks, so they subscribe nothing whatever they override.
pub trait EntityPart: Send + Sync + 'static {
#[doc(hidden)] fn entity_subscribed(&self) -> &'static [Option<HookId>] { &[] }
/// The authoritative tick of one general entity row of type `kind`
/// (`mymod:robot`), with ID `entity`, in the server level's entity phase:
/// `ServerLevel.tickNonPassenger`'s `entity.tick()` for a row the type
/// registry owns (arismoko/horizon#792 stage 4). The row's common bookkeeping has run
/// (its previous position is its current one); a rider is ticked after
/// its vehicle inside the vehicle's own tick, depth-first as
/// `tickPassenger` recurses, and is never ticked as a root. Rows that
/// joined this tick tick from the next, parked levels and rows outside
/// entity-ticking chunks do not tick, and a `entity_tick_pre` that
/// cancels the row cancels its whole rider subtree. Read and change the
/// row's data through its attachments (`event.entity` is their holder);
/// there is no parallel per-entity state. Told only for general entity
/// types: vanilla families tick their own paths (#6509 owns bringing
/// them to one join-order walk), so a part attached to `minecraft:zombie`
/// hears no tick from this hook. Server-side.
/// Side: server.
fn general_entity_tick(&self, host: &mut dyn ServerHost, event: &mut GeneralEntityTickEvent<'_>) { let _ = host;let _ = event;  }

/// The mob's mod goals, added to its two selectors as `GoalSelector.addGoal`
/// does in vanilla's `registerGoals`: fresh at every construction, so a goal's
/// state is never saved. Branch on the mob with `goals.mob()`.
fn register_goals(&self, goals: &mut GoalRegistrar<'_>) { let _ = goals; }
/// Native mods only: entity parts do not cross to wasm yet.
/// The mob's mod brain behaviors, joined into the species' brain beside
/// vanilla's (#824): built wherever the brain is built — its first tick and
/// every rebuild — started and stopped in `Brain.tick`'s order, fresh state
/// each build, nothing saved. Branch on the mob with `behaviors.mob()`.
fn register_behaviors(&self, behaviors: &mut BehaviorRegistrar<'_>) { let _ = behaviors; }
/// How many mobs one spawn group of this mob's type may grow to (`Mob.getMaxSpawnClusterSize`), asked
/// where the spawner has just added a mob to a group and compares the group's size with the limit:
/// natural spawning's packs. `mob` is the mob just spawned, finalized and in
/// no level yet, to read through `host`; `reason` and `spawner` say why and from what it spawned
/// (`spawner` is none but for a spawner block's mobs); `vanilla` is the limit the species has. Answer
/// `Some(limit)` to replace it and `None` to leave it to the next part and then to vanilla's. The
/// first part to answer wins, in dispatch order, and the group stops growing once its size reaches
/// the limit, so a limit of 0 or 1 ends it with the mob just spawned. The host is the read-only
/// one: a listener runs on replayed ticks and must be a pure function of its inputs.
fn spawn_cluster_size(&self, host: &dyn Host, mob: UnspawnedMobRef, reason: SpawnReason, spawner: Option<SpawnSource>, vanilla: u32) -> Option<u32> { let _ = (host, mob, reason, spawner, vanilla); None }
/// Whether `entity` can be sheared now with `shears` (NeoForge's `IShearable.isShearable`), asked
/// where a player's shears (`player` is who holds them) or a dispenser's (`player` none) meet it,
/// before the `player_shear_entity` or `block_shear_entity` hook. The first part to answer wins, in
/// dispatch order: `Some(true)` shears it through [`EntityPart::on_sheared`] in place of the
/// species' own shearing, `Some(false)` leaves it unsheared (a player's click then does nothing),
/// and `None` leaves it to the next part and then to the species. Server-side.
fn is_shearable(&self, host: &dyn Host, entity: EntityRef, player: Option<PlayerRef>, shears: &ItemStack) -> Option<bool> { let _ = (host, entity, player, shears); None }
/// Shears `entity` (NeoForge's `IShearable.onSheared`): called on the part whose
/// [`EntityPart::is_shearable`] said `Some(true)`, once the shear hooks let it through. Change
/// the entity and play its sound here, and return the drops: the game spawns each as
/// `spawnShearedDrop` does (one block above the entity's feet, with a small random push), posts
/// the shear game event and damages the shears. Server-side.
fn on_sheared(&self, host: &mut dyn ServerHost, entity: EntityRef, player: Option<PlayerRef>, shears: &ItemStack) -> Vec<ItemStack> { let _ = (host, entity, player, shears); Vec::new() }
}
/// What [`Hooks::visuals`] is told. Each field is that hook's argument of the same name, described in the hook's doc.
#[allow(missing_docs)]
// no wire: borrowed event crosses as call arguments.
